  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Nomada vincta, commonly known as the Bounded Nomad Bee, is a small cuckoo bee in the Apidae family that parasitizes the nests of other bee species. These solitary bees are found throughout New England and are indicators of healthy native bee communities.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body coloration",
      "description": "Distinctive pattern of black and reddish-brown or yellow markings on the abdomen with clear boundaries between colored bands",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Tiny bee, typically 6-8mm in length, noticeably smaller than honey bees and most bumble bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Sparse body hairs",
      "description": "Relatively smooth appearance with less dense hair coverage compared to other bee species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Nomada vincta thrives in open woodlands, meadows, and gardens throughout New England where host bee species, particularly Andrena bees, establish their nesting sites.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Nomada vincta is a brood parasite that lays its eggs in the nests of host bees, where the nomad larva consumes the host's pollen provisions and eventually destroys the host egg or larva.",
